I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided PXRD workflow documentation, then build and verify a multiphase GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep residual-phase trials in a common refinement state and finish with a fresh replay of the best physical model.

The sample is a 700 °C ZnO + WO₃ reaction measured over 10–100° with Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ and no supplied calibration. I’m now screening the frozen reference library for Zn–W–O candidates and extracting the observed peak/residual structure before committing to phase identities.

The dominant peaks identify wolframite-type ZnWO₄, while separate peak sets match monoclinic WO₃ and wurtzite ZnO. Because Zn–W–O entries are intentionally absent from the available library, I found a physically sound P2/c CoMoO₄ analogue (two substitutions, unlike the geometrically poor FeWO₄ entry) and am retaining P2/c, P‑1, scheelite, and fergusonite target-composition variants for common-state comparison.

The first three-phase common fit confirms all three phases have positive, stable scales (roughly 77% ZnWO₄, 20% WO₃, 3% ZnO), but the nuisance refinement is not yet trustworthy: zero and specimen displacement became nearly singular and the profile stalled near 20% Rwp. I’m freezing that interpretation and diagnosing the residual/peak-position correlations before refining structure or reporting fractions.

The [100] March–Dollase model is decisive for ZnWO₄ (Rwp fell from 20.2% to 10.7%, ratio ≈0.75); WO₃ and ZnO texture effects are much smaller. Refining the transferred P2/c coordinates and physically constrained phase-wide Uiso values brings the fit to ~8.95% Rwp. I rejected unconstrained per-atom ADPs and full WO₃ oxygen refinement because they produced negative/very large ADPs or implausibly large oxygen shifts despite a lower residual.

The three-phase model is stable: wolframite ZnWO₄ dominates, with monoclinic WO₃ and wurtzite ZnO as crystalline residuals. I’m now testing the remaining nuisance/profile choices and alternative WO₃/ZnO structures in the same multiphase state, then I’ll replay and export the best physically valid project.

The alternative monoclinic WO₃ reference improves the common-state fit from 8.64% to 8.21% Rwp, while orthorhombic and high-pressure models are decisively worse. A triclinic description is close but less stable. I’m checking whether the improved monoclinic coordinates and bond geometry remain physical before adopting that structure.

The adopted WO₃ model has sensible W–O octahedra (1.73–2.16 Å) and no short O–O contacts. Isotropic profile refinement yields only a small, stable gain for ZnWO₄; WO₃ is effectively unchanged, and ZnO’s size parameter is not independently resolvable. I’m screening only the physically allowed uniaxial microstrain axes now, rather than adding unconstrained profile terms.

The closure refinement is now at 8.10% Rwp with all phase-wide displacement parameters positive and physical. The refined crystalline fractions are converging near 77.5% ZnWO₄, 17.8% WO₃, and 4.7% ZnO. I’m doing one final coordinate-sensitivity test for WO₃; it will be retained only if the oxygen shifts and octahedral geometry remain credible.

The final candidate has converged at 8.096% project Rwp with three positive, physical structures. I’ve frozen that state into `final.gpx`; the next check regenerates every reflection and the calculated profile from the saved atoms and profile parameters on the full original grid, ignoring cached calculated intensities.

The independent replay passed cleanly: all 8301 points were regenerated with zero optimized parameters, the project remained unchanged, and there were no negative-background, peak-width, or displacement-tensor flags. The exported CIFs also pass the occupancy/contact screen and match the structures embedded in `final.gpx` exactly.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

- ZnWO₄ (wolframite, P2/c): 77.4883 wt%
- WO₃ (monoclinic, P2₁/c): 17.8253 wt%
- ZnO (wurtzite, P6₃mc): 4.6864 wt%

